course: dictionary
lesson: 5746
title: Louse: a tiny insect, a rude word, and a spoiler
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one fits. «Head lice are tiny insects that live in hair» shows that a louse is a small insect that lives on people. The sentence around the word gives you the clue.
explain: One louse, many lice. «Head lice spread through shared combs, brushes and hats» uses the plural lice, because more than one insect is meant. Check the meaning by swapping it into the sentence: does «tiny insects» still make sense? It does, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Slang and informal words change the meaning again. If someone shouts «You louse!» they are calling you a nasty, mean person, which is rude. If someone says «You really louse up the game», they mean you spoiled it. Look at the situation, then ask: which meaning fits?
fact: A louse is a tiny insect without wings that lives on the skin or hair of people or animals and feeds on blood.
fact: The plural of louse is lice. «Head lice» are common, and «they are not a sign of being dirty».
fact: «Head lice» spread through shared combs, brushes and hats, and touching heads.
fact: Calling someone a louse is slang and rude, meaning a nasty, mean person.
fact: To louse up means to spoil or mess something up, which is an informal phrase.
exercise: «Head lice are tiny insects that live in hair.» What is a louse? | *A tiny insect without wings that lives on skin or hair | A small fish that swims in rivers | A type of hair ribbon | A large bird that nests in trees
exercise: «A fine comb gets head lice out, and clean hair gets lice just as easily as dirty hair.» What does lice mean here? | To mess something up | A special kind of comb | One nasty, mean person | *More than one louse, tiny insects
exercise: Sam says, «You really louse up the game, Sam!» What does louse up mean? | To buy a brand new game | To hide under a bed | *To spoil or mess something up | To win the game easily
test: In slang, calling someone «a louse» means they are | A kind neighbour | A tiny wingless insect | *A nasty, mean person | A very fast runner
test: Which tool helps get head lice out of hair? | A kitchen spoon | A paint brush | A pair of scissors | *A fine comb
exam: In «Head lice spread through shared combs, brushes and hats», what does louse mean? | A nasty, mean person | A type of hat | *A tiny insect that lives in hair | To spoil something
